\section{Materialize Virtual Objects Once on a Side Exit}

A speculative optimizer may keep the fields of a short-lived record in registers rather than allocate the record, provided its identity and allocation are unobservable during that execution. If a guard later fails, the interpreter may need the object to continue, including any aliases to it. Model an exit description as a graph of virtual object identifiers, with layouts and field recipes, and a list of recipes for interpreter slots. A recipe refers to a virtual identifier or to an already available value; a virtual identity has no corresponding allocated object yet. Assume that field construction itself runs no user code and that allocation failure is outside the modeled semantics. On success the reconstructed frame must have the same values and identity relations as the general frame.

\begin{verbatim}
materialize(desc, live):
  map = empty root table
  ids = reachable_virtual_ids(desc.slots, desc.fields)
  for id in ids:
    # Reserve one rooted object per logical identity.
    map[id] = allocate_blank(desc.layout[id])
  for id in ids:
    for (field, recipe) in desc.fields[id]:
      # Forward and backward references now have targets.
      set_field(map[id], field, resolve(recipe, live, map))
  slots = resolve_all(desc.slots, live, map)
  return interpreter_frame(desc.resume_pc, slots)
\end{verbatim}

For an exit with slots \(x\) and \(y\) both naming virtual object \(A\), let \(A\) contain the integer \(6\) and a link to virtual object \(B\), and let \(B\)'s link point back to \(A\). The first pass allocates two objects, say at addresses \(40\) and \(72\); the second installs links \(40\to72\to40\). A resumed test comparing \(x\) and \(y\) returns true. After writing \(9\) into the integer field through \(x\), a read through \(y\) returns \(9\). Allocating once per slot would produce distinct objects for \(x\) and \(y\), changing both observations.

\Cref{fig:visual-170} shows the restored identity graph. Two
slot references to \(A\) are not two requests for allocation.

